

📘 DFDL Schema Breakdown – Short Notes

🔹 Purpose

DFDL annotations define how data (text/binary) should be parsed and serialized using rules inside an XSD structure.


---

🔹 Example Schema (Simplified)

<xs:complexType name="myNumbers">
  <xs:sequence 
      dfdl:separator=";" 
      dfdl:encoding="ascii">

    <xs:element name="myInt" type="xs:int"
        dfdl:representation="text"
        dfdl:textNumberRep="standard"
        dfdl:encoding="ascii"
        dfdl:lengthKind="delimited"
        dfdl:initiator="int=" />

    <xs:element name="myFloat" type="xs:float"
        dfdl:representation="text"
        dfdl:textNumberRep="standard"
        dfdl:encoding="ascii"
        dfdl:lengthKind="delimited"
        dfdl:initiator="float=" />

  </xs:sequence>
</xs:complexType>


---

🔹 Breakdown & Meaning

Property	Description

dfdl:separator=";"	Elements separated by semicolon (;)
dfdl:initiator="int=" / "float="	Start marker before each element
dfdl:representation="text"	Data stored in text form (not binary)
dfdl:textNumberRep="standard"	Numbers follow normal format (e.g., 5, -7.1E8)
dfdl:lengthKind="delimited"	Element ends with delimiter (no fixed length)
dfdl:encoding="ascii"	Data encoded using ASCII



---

🔹 Input Example

int=5;float=-7.1E8

🔹 Output after DFDL Parsing

<myNumbers>
   <myInt>5</myInt>
   <myFloat>-7.1E8</myFloat>
</myNumbers>


---

🔹 Summary

Concept	Explanation

XSD elements	Define logical structure (like myInt, myFloat)
DFDL annotations	Define physical format (delimiter, encoding, initiator, etc.)
Result	Parser converts raw text → structured XML tree



---

🟢 In short:
DFDL = XSD + Physical Format Rules
Example → "int=5;float=-7.1E8" gets parsed to a clean XML tree using annotations.


